.TH CVEC_RESERVE 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_reserve \- reserve capacity for future elements (type-inferred, fatal on failure)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_reserve(v, new_capacity_count);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro is a wrapper for cvector_reserve(). It calls ccol_fatal_err()
when the reservation fails. A caller with no useful recovery for an
allocation failure therefore does not need to test a return value. A
reservation that is too large to represent is also a failure. See NOTES for
the two limits that the macro tests it against.

The capacity grows to the next power of two at or above new_capacity_count,
with a floor of 4 elements. The reserved capacity is therefore usually
larger than the request. A request at or below the current capacity does
nothing. This call never shrinks the capacity. The macro does not change
the element count. The new space is capacity and not elements. You can
reach it only after a push into it.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v                   Vector to reserve capacity for
new_capacity_count  Minimum number of elements to reserve
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() when the reservation
fails. The reservation fails when the allocation fails. It fails when the
capacity is more than ccol_max_elem_count. It also fails when the
capacity multiplied by the element size overflows a size_t.
The macro tests both limits against the rounded-up capacity. It does not
test them against new_capacity_count itself. A request inside a limit can
therefore still fail after the round-up to the next power of two. A
vector of wide elements reaches the size_t overflow limit first. That
limit bounds the capacity at SIZE_MAX divided by the element size. For
any element wider than one byte, that is the lower limit of the two.
The macro evaluates new_capacity_count exactly once, on every path. This
includes the failure path. An expression with a side effect is therefore
safe to pass.
The macro rounds the real capacity up to a power of two, with a minimum
of 4.
The element count stays the same. Use cvec_size(3) to read it.
A reservation invalidates every pointer from an earlier
cvec_data_ptr(3) or cvec_at_ptr(3).
The macro asserts when v is NULL.
Use cvector_reserve(3) directly to handle a failed reservation yourself.
That function does not stop the program.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_reserve(vec, 100);   // capacity becomes 128, size stays 0
for (int i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
    cvec_push(vec, i);   // no reallocation in the loop
}
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_reserve (3),
.BR cvec_append_array (3),
.BR cvec_push (3)
